# merlint configuration (#153).
#
# Warn-first adoption: CI reports findings but does not block the build yet.
# The excluded rules below conflict with deliberate project conventions; every
# other rule stays enabled. See MERLINT.md for the full rationale and the
# tracked follow-ups (catch-all audit, long-function refactor).
#
# Excluded rules and why:
#   E205 - we use Printf/Format throughout, not the Fmt module.
#   E320 - long, descriptive identifiers (e.g. fk_child_has_ref_multi_in_tx)
#          are intentional and aid readability over terse names.
#   E410 - our doc-comment shape differs from merlint's preferred odoc style.
#   E600/E605/E606/E610 - test-layout rules. Our suite is behavior-oriented
#          (a single test file exercises many library modules) and lives flat
#          under test/; it is not a 1:1 mirror of lib/ modules. These fire on
#          every test file (and E605 on lib modules) by design, not as defects.
#
# Scoped to test/ only (#167, #168):
#   E105 - catch-all exception handlers. In tests these are idiomatic best-effort
#          cleanup (temp-file teardown `(try Unix.unlink p with _ -> ())`,
#          Fun.protect finalizers, env-var parse fallbacks); a swallowed error in
#          test scaffolding just skips cleanup. E105 stays ENABLED for lib/ and
#          bin/, where the 12 production sites were narrowed to precise exceptions
#          (Unix.Unix_error / Failure / Parser.Error / Invalid_argument).
#   E005 - long functions. merlint auto-skips functions in NESTED test dirs but
#          our suite lives flat under test/ (dirname has no '/'), so long bench
#          harnesses (open_slow_store, main, open_slow_wal) are not auto-skipped.
#          Long test/bench setup is acceptable; E005 stays ENABLED for lib/+bin/,
#          which is where the #168 refactor drives it down.
#
# Multiple file blocks union (rule_config.should_exclude is List.exists), so the
# test/ block layers E105 on top of the global exclusions for test files.
#
# Patterns are unquoted: merlint's config parser does not strip surrounding
# quotes, so "**/*.ml*" would be taken literally.

rules:
  - files: **/*.ml*
    exclude: [E205, E320, E410, E600, E605, E606, E610]
  - files: test/*.ml*
    exclude: [E105, E005]
